          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong
              universe of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            is a realm within which (some conceived part, naively and virtually all, of) the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            may be thought of as taking place. Universes can be purely metamathematical, but we can also reflect upon them and bring them into mathematics.
          %h2#categories_as_universes Categories as universes
          %p
            Much of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+mathematics"} ordinary mathematics
            can be thought of as taking place
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} inside
            “the archetypical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} SET
            of sets”. Typically, the properties of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SET
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SET
            are formulated in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+logic"} first-order logic
            using a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            or (more directly)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
          %p
            We can generalise this from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SET
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SET
            to any other
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalisation"} internalisation
            %em specific
            category is merely one
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
            %em class
            of categories is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
            (really a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/doctrine"} doctrine
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-theory"} 2-theory
          %p
            We can also use
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher categories
            instead of mere categories here. Even for ordinary mathematics, this means starting with
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-Grpd"} GRPD
            instead of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SET
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SET
          %h2#universes_as_classes Universes as classes
          %p
            The idea of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+category"} large category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SET
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SET
            as the universe of mathematics has an analogue in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class+theory"} class theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+class+theory"} material class theory
            %strong von Neumann universe
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class"} class
            of all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+foundation"} well-founded
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+sets"} pure sets
          %p
            More explicitly: for every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+number"} ordinal number
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\alpha
            (the von Neumann universe of
            %strong rank
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/recursion"} recursively
            using the operations of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
            and (material)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/union"} union
            as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi α
                  %mo ≔
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                    %mrow
                      %mi β
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi α
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi β
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\alpha \coloneqq \bigcup_{\beta \lt \alpha} \mathcal{P}V_\beta .
          %p
            Then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            itself is the union of all of the
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\alpha
          %p
            A similar but more complicated definition allows us to define the universe
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            of
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructible+set"} constructible set
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructible+universe"} constructible universe
          %p
            See also a
            %a{:href => "https://secure.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/wiki/Universe_%28mathematics%29"} Wikipedia article
            written largely by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Toby+Bartels"} Toby Bartels
            in another lifetime.
          %p
            In a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+class+theory"} structural class theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+class+structure"} category with class structure
            found in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+set+theory"} algebraic set theory
            %strong universe
            is a class
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monic"} monic
            class
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/map"} map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}(U) \hookrightarrow U
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+object"} powerclass
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+class"} universal class
            is a universe.
          %h2#grothendieck_universes Grothendieck universes
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+universe"} Grothendieck universe
            is a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            such that the following properties hold for
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
          %ol
            %li
              if
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in a \in U
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in U
            %li
              if
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b \in U
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{a, b\}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ×
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\times b
              are elements of
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %li
              if
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in U
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∪
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cup a
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(a)
              are elements of
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %li
              the set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
              of all natural numbers is an element of
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %li
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi a
                    %mo →
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:a \to b
              is surjective with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in U
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \subseteq U
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in U
          %h2#universes_inside_
            Universes inside
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SET
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SET
          %p
            If set theory is the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundation+of+mathematics"} foundation of mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+categories"} small categories
            or sets that exist within set theory and have the properties of a universe.
          %p
            There is already a hint of this in the hierarchy
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\alpha
            out of which the von Neumann universe is built. For some values of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\alpha
            might be a sufficiently large and complete collection of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            in which to do ordinary mathematics. From the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nPOV"} nPOV
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Set
                    %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set_\alpha
            of these sets and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            between them, although it's more common to think about
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Set
                    %mi κ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set_\kappa
            for some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinal+number"} cardinal number
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
          %p
            An
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite+set"} infinite set
            is a simple example, as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+mathematics"} finite mathematics
            can be done inside it. Going beyond this, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+universe"} Grothendieck universe
            is a set within which other infinite sets exist but which is complete under the operations of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            that are needed for ordinary mathematics. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural
            analogue is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe+in+the+topos"} universe in the topos
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SET
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SET
          %p
            In general, we need some axioms to state the existence of such universes; we can think of these as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+cardinal"} large cardinal
            axioms. (The existence of an infinite set is the
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+infinity"} axiom of infinity
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inaccessible+cardinal"} inaccessible cardinal
          %p
            The use of such universes is convenient when we want to work with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+categories"} large categories
            “as if they were small.” That is, if we redefine “small” to mean “element of some fixed universe,” then the categories of all small structures of some sort will still be sets (rather than
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+classes"} proper classes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+categories"} functor categories
            between them freely. We do sometimes then need a way to “move a category” from one universe to another; see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe+enlargement"} universe enlargement
          %h2#reflection Reflection
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflection+principle"} reflection principle
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "https://secure.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/wiki/Reflection_principle"} Wikipedia
          %p This follows the following outline:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                We assume that we understand
                %em a priori
                what it means to use
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+logic"} first-order logic
                (or some other finitary base logic).
            %li
              %p
                Using this base logic, we can formulate a foundational
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
                that describes a meta-universe such as
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi SET
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SET
            %li
              %p
                Of course,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi SET
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SET
                cannot be described from inside itself. But there may be objects in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi SET
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SET
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+categories"}> internal categories
                ) that
                %em look like
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi SET
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SET
                itself.
            %li
              %p
                We add an additional axiom to our set theory stating that such objects, the
                = succeed "," do
                  %em internal universes
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/models"} models
                of our original set theory.
            %li
              %p Now we are using a new, stronger set theory; repeat.
          %h2#universe_of_sets_in_type_theory Universe of sets in type theory
          %p
            Set theory is not the only
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundation+of+mathematics"} foundation of mathematics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theories"} type theories
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            to get a meta-universe of sets.
          %p
            We can also add axioms for the existence of internal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universes"} type universes
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            similarly isolate the types which are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-sets"} h-sets
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Set
                      %mi U
                    %mo ≔
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo :
                        %mi U
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isSet
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Set}_U \coloneqq \sum_{A:U} \mathrm{isSet}(A)
            = succeed "." do
              %strong universe of sets
          %p
            See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universe"} type universe
            for the more general concept of a universe of types.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructible+universe"} constructible universe
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe+enlargement"} universe enlargement
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe+polymorphism"} universe polymorphism
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+of+types"} type of types
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"}> subobject classifier
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object+classifier"} object classifier
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+map+classifier"} partial map classifier
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subuniverse"} reflective subuniverse
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-universe"} G-universe
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p
            For universes in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class+theory"} class theory
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+set+theory"} algebraic set theory
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Awodey"}> Steve Awodey
              \.
              = succeed "," do
                %em Notes on algebraic set theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.phil.cmu.edu/projects/ast/Papers/bnotes.pdf"} pdf
          %p For Grothendieck’s early use of the term
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"}> Alexander Grothendieck
              \.
              = succeed "," do
                %em Sur la formalisation des catégories et foncteurs
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://csg.igrothendieck.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/U2u.pdf"} pdf
          %h3#in_type_theory In type theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Hofmann"}> Martin Hofmann
                , section 2.1.6 of
                %em Syntax and semantics of dependent types
                (
                %a{:href => "http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/summary?doi=10.1.1.36.8985"}> web
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Univalent+Foundations+Project"}> Univalent Foundations Project
                ,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HoTT+book"} Homotopy Type Theory – Univalent Foundations of Mathematics
                (2013)
